---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import TxgPipeline from "../../components/TxgPipeline.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import RoomNav from "../../components/RoomNav.astro";
import { gh } from "../../lib/openzfs";
import { room, roomUrl, roomNumber } from "../../lib/rooms";

const lang = "en";
const r = room("txg");
const L = {
  granularity: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 44, 46),
  three: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 47, 52),
  concurrent: gh("include/sys/txg.h", 41),
  size: gh("include/sys/txg.h", 42, 45),
  timeout: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 116),
  flush: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 2085, 2098),
  labels: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 2109, 2154),
  assign: gh("module/zfs/dmu_tx.c", 1085),
  hold: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 333, 338),
  commit: gh("module/zfs/dmu_tx.c", 1460),
  rele: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 372, 375),
  count: gh("include/sys/txg_impl.h", 75),
  state: gh("include/sys/txg_impl.h", 94, 103),
  startSync: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 601),
  askQuiesce: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 568, 577),
  waitSynced: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 714, 717),
  defer: gh("include/sys/txg.h", 49),
  ioctl: gh("module/zfs/zfs_ioctl.c", 7306),
  kick: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 830, 832),
  dirtySpace: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 997, 1002),
  dirtySync: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 983, 987),
  syncPct: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 120),
  dirtyMax: gh("module/zfs/dsl_pool.c", 99, 105),
  waitOpen: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 791, 794),
  longFree: gh("module/zfs/dmu.c", 1096, 1106),
  converge: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 92, 99),
  rewrite: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 128),
  deferred: gh("module/zfs/zio.c", 122),
  rewriteConfig: gh("module/zfs/spa.c", 10958),
  minVdevs: gh("module/zfs/spa.c", 10792, 10815),
  noChange: gh("module/zfs/vdev_label.c", 2071, 2077),
  ubChanged: gh("module/zfs/uberblock.c", 74),
  fsync: gh("module/zfs/zfs_vnops.c", 117, 120),
  perCpu: gh("include/sys/txg_impl.h", 51, 55),
  threads: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 210, 219),
  scanActive: gh("module/zfs/dsl_scan.c", 3723, 3732),
  passes: gh("module/zfs/txg.c", 86, 90),
};
---

<Base
  lang={lang}
  roomId="txg"
  title="Transaction groups · Uberblok"
  description="How ZFS batches every change into numbered transaction groups, open, quiescing and syncing, and commits each with one uberblock write: interactive, with the OpenZFS code."
>
  <article class="wrap">
    <header class="room-head">
      <p class="kicker">Room {roomNumber(r.id)}</p>
      <h1>{r.title.en}</h1>
      <div class="prose problem">
        <p class="lead">
          Copy-on-write keeps the disks consistent at every instant, but only if the switch from the old tree to the new
          one is a single write. Writes never stop arriving, from many threads at once. If every one of them ended with
          its own commit, the pool would flush every disk and rewrite its labels and uberblock for every single
          write. And a commit has to cover a state in which no change is half done.
        </p>
        <p>
          ZFS answers with batches. Every change joins a numbered <em>transaction group</em>, a txg, and ZFS
          <a href={L.granularity}>“asserts consistency at the granularity of these transaction groups”</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="pipeline">
      <h2 id="pipeline"><span class="n">1</span> Three txgs, one per stage</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          A txg is <a href={L.three}>open, quiescing or syncing</a>, and there is never more than one txg in each state:
          <a href={L.concurrent}><code>TXG_CONCURRENT_STATES</code> is 3</a>. Writes go into the open txg. To commit it, a
          thread closes it and opens the next one at once, so writers never wait for a disk. It then waits for the
          writes still under way in the closed txg to finish. A second thread writes the closed txg out and ends with the
          uberblock. Add writes, play, or go one event at a time.
        </p>
      </div>
      <TxgPipeline lang={lang} />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="depths">
      <h2 id="depths">The idea, at three depths</h2>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one sentence</h3>
        <p class="lead prose">
          ZFS gathers every change into a numbered transaction group, closes it once no writer is still inside, and writes
          it out as a whole, so one uberblock write commits thousands of changes at a moment when none is half done.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In one paragraph</h3>
        <p class="prose">
          Every change to the pool is a transaction that joins the open txg. <a href={L.threads}>Two kernel threads per
          pool</a> move txgs along. The sync thread wakes when <a href={L.timeout}><code>zfs_txg_timeout</code></a> (5 seconds) has passed
          since the last sync began, or sooner when someone needs a sync: <code>zpool sync</code>, or a txg that holds too
          much dirty data. It asks the quiesce thread for the open txg. The quiesce thread opens the next txg, waits until
          no transaction holds the old one, and hands it over. The sync thread then writes it out in passes until nothing
          is left to write, and commits it with the uberblock. Meanwhile the next txg is already taking writes. A txg in
          which nothing changed writes nothing, not even an uberblock. A program that needs its data on disk at once,
          with <code>fsync</code>, normally does not wait for a txg: it <a href={L.fsync}>goes through the ZIL</a>
          (room 10).
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="depth">
        <h3>In detail</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            <strong>Joining the open txg.</strong> <code>dmu_tx_assign</code> <a href={L.assign}>calls
            <code>txg_hold_open</code></a>, which reads <code>tx_open_txg</code> and
            <a href={L.hold}>raises a counter of the CPU it runs on</a>, <code>tc_count[txg &amp; TXG_MASK]</code>.
            <a href={L.size}>Per-txg state is kept in arrays of <code>TXG_SIZE</code> = 4</a>, indexed by the low two bits
            of the txg number, so four consecutive txgs never share a slot. When the transaction is done,
            <code>dmu_tx_commit</code> <a href={L.commit}>calls <code>txg_rele_to_sync</code></a>, which
            <a href={L.rele}>lowers the counter</a> and wakes the quiesce thread when it reaches 0. The counters are per CPU
            <a href={L.perCpu}>because a single one “would result in heavy lock contention”</a>; the figure draws
            <a href={L.count}>these counters</a> for 4 CPUs and the <a href={L.state}>txg numbers of each state</a>
            above them.
          </p>
          <p><strong>When a txg ends.</strong> The sync thread decides, in its wait loop.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/txg.c"
          from={532}
          to={553}
          symbol="txg_sync_thread"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 533,
              text: `The timeout is <code>zfs_txg_timeout</code> seconds, <a href="${L.timeout}">5 by default</a>, in clock ticks.`,
            },
            {
              from: 542,
              text: `<code>timer</code> counts from <code>start</code>, set when <a href="${L.startSync}">the previous <code>spa_sync</code> began</a>: the 5 seconds run from the start of the last sync, not from its end.`,
            },
            {
              from: 543,
              to: 546,
              text: `The thread sleeps while all of these hold: no scan is active (<a href="${L.scanActive}">a scrub, a resilver, or a background destroy or free</a>), the timer has not run out, nobody waits for a txg past the last synced one, and no quiesced txg is waiting. Then it <a href="${L.askQuiesce}">asks the quiesce thread for the open txg</a>.`,
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Two kinds of callers raise <code>tx_sync_txg_waiting</code>. <code>zpool sync</code> ends in
            <a href={L.ioctl}><code>txg_wait_synced(dp, 0)</code></a>, which <a href={L.waitSynced}>waits for the open txg
            plus <code>TXG_DEFER_SIZE</code></a>, <a href={L.defer}>2</a>: in the figure, it syncs three txgs in a row.
            And each change adds its size to the dirty data of its txg; past
            <a href={L.syncPct}><code>zfs_dirty_data_sync_percent</code>, 20%</a>, of
            <a href={L.dirtyMax}><code>zfs_dirty_data_max</code></a> (by default 10% of memory, capped),
            <a href={L.dirtySpace}><code>dsl_pool_dirty_space</code></a> <a href={L.dirtySync}>sees it</a> and calls
            <a href={L.kick}><code>txg_kick</code></a>, so a busy pool syncs well before 5 seconds.
          </p>
          <p><strong>Closing it.</strong> The quiesce thread runs <code>txg_quiesce</code>.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/txg.c"
          from={395}
          to={428}
          symbol="txg_quiesce"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 398,
              to: 399,
              text: "Taking every CPU’s <code>tc_open_lock</code> keeps new writers out of this txg: <code>txg_hold_open</code> takes the lock of its CPU before it reads <code>tx_open_txg</code>.",
            },
            {
              from: 402,
              text: "The open txg number goes up by one: from here on, new writes join the next txg.",
            },
            {
              from: 412,
              to: 413,
              text: "The locks are released at once. The next txg is open before the old one is drained, so writers do not wait.",
            },
            {
              from: 422,
              to: 428,
              text: "For each CPU in turn, wait until its count for this txg is 0. Writes that joined before the switch finish here. When the loop ends, nothing can change the txg any more.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p><strong>One txg per state.</strong> The quiesce thread’s loop is where the rule is kept.</p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/txg.c"
          from={628}
          to={667}
          symbol="txg_quiesce_thread"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 631,
              to: 641,
              text: "It sleeps until someone asks for a txg past the open one, and as long as the last txg it quiesced has not been taken by the sync thread. One txg at most is quiescing or quiesced.",
            },
            {
              from: 646,
              to: 654,
              text: "It takes the open txg, records it in <code>tx_quiescing_txg</code>, and runs <code>txg_quiesce</code> with the pool’s sync lock released.",
            },
            {
              from: 662,
              to: 666,
              text: "The hand-off: the txg moves to <code>tx_quiesced_txg</code> and the sync thread is woken. If the sync thread is still writing the previous txg, this one waits there, quiesced.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Who asks? Normally the sync thread itself, when it is ready for more work. So while one txg syncs, the next
            usually stays open and keeps taking writes. One caller asks early:
            <a href={L.waitOpen}><code>txg_wait_open(dp, 0, B_TRUE)</code></a>, which at this tag
            <a href={L.longFree}><code>dmu_free_long_range</code> calls</a> “to avoid filling up a TXG with just frees”.
            Then the next txg quiesces during the sync, and the three states are busy at once: in the figure, “Free a
            large file” while a txg syncs.
          </p>
          <p><strong>The hand-off to the sync thread.</strong></p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/txg.c"
          from={583}
          to={610}
          symbol="txg_sync_thread"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 588,
              to: 593,
              text: "The sync thread takes the quiesced txg, records it as <code>tx_syncing_txg</code>, and wakes the quiesce thread, which can now quiesce another txg if one was asked for.",
            },
            {
              from: 601,
              to: 603,
              text: "<code>spa_sync</code> does all the I/O of the txg. <code>start</code> is the moment the timeout of the next wait counts from.",
            },
            {
              from: 607,
              to: 610,
              text: "The txg is synced: <code>tx_synced_txg</code>, and everyone in <code>txg_wait_synced</code> is woken.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            <strong>Writing until nothing changes.</strong> Writing a block allocates space, which changes a space map,
            which is metadata in the MOS, which then has to be written too. So <code>spa_sync</code> runs passes. <a href={L.passes}>The first
            writes all the modified user data; the next ones write only metadata</a>, what the previous pass dirtied.
            <a href={L.converge}>To make it converge</a>, from <a href={L.rewrite}>pass 2</a> a block born in this txg is
            rewritten in place instead of reallocated (the copy-on-write room shows where), and from
            <a href={L.deferred}>pass 2</a> frees are deferred to a later txg (without the log spacemap feature).
          </p>
        </div>
        <Excerpt
          lang={lang}
          path="module/zfs/spa.c"
          from={10743}
          to={10766}
          symbol="spa_sync_iterate_to_convergence"
          notes={[
            {
              from: 10749,
              to: 10764,
              text: "If pass 1 finds nothing to write, the root block pointer was not born in this txg and the MOS is clean: the txg is a no-op, and the loop stops.",
            },
            {
              from: 10765,
              text: "Otherwise, another pass as long as the MOS is dirty in this txg. The loop ends when a pass changes nothing.",
            },
          ]}
        />
        <div class="prose after">
          <p>
            Then <a href={L.rewriteConfig}><code>spa_sync_rewrite_vdev_config</code></a> calls
            <code>vdev_config_sync</code>, which <a href={L.flush}>flushes the write cache of every disk written in this
            txg</a>, then <a href={L.labels}>writes the even labels, the uberblock and the odd labels</a>. The labels are
            rewritten only on the vdevs whose configuration changed in this txg. The uberblock goes to every top-level vdev
            if the configuration changed, else to <a href={L.minVdevs}>up to three top-level vdevs</a>, taken in order from a
            random one, and then it is the only new thing in their labels. The
            uberblock write is the commit, and its cost (a flush per disk, label writes) is paid once per txg, not once per
            write. If nothing changed in the txg, the uberblock <a href={L.ubChanged}>reports no change</a> and
            <a href={L.noChange}>nothing is written</a> (unless multihost protection is on): an idle pool goes through a
            txg every 5 seconds without writing to its disks. The <a href={roomUrl(lang, "hall")}>Hall</a> shows where the uberblock lands in the ring.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section simplified" aria-labelledby="simplified">
      <h2 id="simplified">What the figure leaves out</h2>
      <p class="prose muted">
        The figure runs the two threads’ wait conditions as the code writes them, over the same variables. It draws 4
        CPUs; each write is one block, and the dirty-data threshold is scaled to 4 blocks (20% of a 20-block
        <code>zfs_dirty_data_max</code>). Time is slowed, and the length of a write or a pass is made up. It leaves out
        the write throttle (writers delayed or blocked as dirty data grows), the wait on <code>tc_open_lock</code> while a
        txg closes, scrubs and resilvers, synctasks, the ZIL, and pool suspension. Pass sizes are illustrative: a txg with
        data always takes three passes here.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
  <RoomNav lang={lang} roomId="txg" />
</Base>

